{
  "what_this_found": "21 fixtures in this tree call importorskip, which makes the skip as broad as the widest dependency of anything in their file rather than what the tests beneath them use. Of the 23 fixture-and-module pairs this environment can decide, 3 switch off tests that provably never touch the module, 61 tests in total.",
  "what_it_costs_today": "0 of those 3 are costing coverage in CI now. CI installs the modules the others are gated on, so those tests run today and go quiet the moment one of those dependencies moves.",
  "where_this_came_from": "A review lane found two tests switched off everywhere by this pattern in PR 1011: the fixture they shared asked for Open3D to satisfy a module-scope import that only the harness's main() needed, and CI installs no Open3D. One of the two was the test validating that change's own headline reading. Blocking the import the way real absence does, that file went from 20 passed and 2 skipped to 23 passed. That site is fixed in its own branch; this measures how far the shape reaches.",
  "what_this_does_not_establish": "Not that the tests are good, only that they run. Not that a module is unused by the file -- several of these fixtures sit beside tests that do need the dependency, which is why the measurement is per test rather than per file. And not a complete census: a pair this environment cannot decide is reported as undecided, not as clean.",
  "what_this_measures": "Whether a test skipped for a missing module actually uses that module. A test that skips when the module is absent and passes when the module is importable but unusable never touched it, so the skip was switching off a test that had nothing to do with the dependency.",
  "why_a_pass_under_poison_is_the_only_evidence_counted": "Poisoning a module that other libraries import during collection can break collection for reasons unrelated to the test, so a failure is not evidence of need. Only a pass is counted, which makes every number here a lower bound.",
